A||||9||merlin||Grand Am brakes Machine Shop Costs||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||01-09-2001||09:02 PM||||I'm probably going to be converting my front brakes to the Grand Am brakes this winter.  For people that have done this mod., what is the machine shop costs that people have paid to remove the disk part of the rotor?  I've heard of everything from $40 to $125. What can I expect to pay?...<P>...or should I just get the 'Grand Am' brake kit from the Fiero Store rather than dealing with the machine shop.<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||Will||01-10-2001||10:14 AM||||Well, convetional wisdom on this conversion isn't that wise.<P>Assuming you've read the article at <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html</A>    I will say that there are several things wrong with it.<P>My father works at a machine shop, so getting machine work done is easy for me.<P>However, when the hubs were machined, we found that the hole through the beretta rotor is 1/16" larger than the shoulder on which it needs to locate.<BR>That means that a concentricity ring needs to be made to up make sure that the rotor is properly centered radially.<BR>If the 10 lb rotor is not centered, even if it is off by only 1/16", it will give you noticeable vibration at speed.<BR>The inside diameter of the hat section of the beretta rotors can not accurately be used to locate because it is an "as cast" surface. That diameter will be different fo different rotors. Even if you can center your rotors on that inside diamter (ID), then other beretta rotors will not fit properly.<P><BR>The locating shoulder also locates the wheel. The thickness of the beretta rotor is close to the height of this shoulder, so that when the beretta rotor is on the hub, the wheel is not well located, producing further vibration at speed.<P>There is a picture of the machined hub at <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/lathecut/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/lathecut/index.html</A>    This is also NOT the way you want things done. In this picture the hat section of the original Fiero rotor is still in place, even though the rotor has been removed. The hat section must also be removed or it will block the passage of air into the beretta rotors, negating the entire purpose of doing the conversion: having vented front rotors.<P>The mounting surface of the Fiero hub should also be faced down by half the thickness of the beretta rotor hat section in order for the beretta rotor to be centered axially where the old Fiero rotor was centered. This also reduces the increase in scrub radius from doing this conversion.<P>That's my take on it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HarryG||01-10-2001||12:24 PM||||Will: you raise a lot of valid points.<BR>Got any pictures of your conversion?<P>Merlin: finding a shop to do "one-offs" is getting harder and harder.  All the guys with CNC equipment want to know is "how many hundred pieces do you want?" <BR>You might check with the guys in your area who are into street rods; they usually know where to get special jobs done.  Maybe you can find a retired machinist or hobbyist who has a mill and lathe in his basement!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||01-10-2001||03:59 PM||||I'm still doing my conversion. Yes I have pcitures (Digital ones, even!), but I don't have a webpage yet...||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||01-10-2001||04:28 PM||||Back to the original question, I paid $50 for the machine work about 1-1/2 years ago. Probably up to $75 would still be reasonable in my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||01-10-2001||07:23 PM||||I don't much like the way many people have done the machine work. I have the same reservations about centering. In addition to the machine issues..... I ran the numbers on the Hydraulics today. The results were interesting.<P>As most people here have read, you can do this with the OE master cyl but you get really long pedal travel. To fix that the MC from a full size blazer is used instead.<P>The ratio of piston areas for the 2 setups is very close to the same. The Blazer MC & Grand Am caliper has a very slight increase in ratio. You can, for now, get the excel sheet I did the math on here. <A HREF="http://theogre.home.sprynet.com/brake_math.xls" TARGET=_blank>Download/view brake_math.xls</A> It will probably be integrated into a cave article soon so this link may only be good for a limited time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||merlin||01-11-2001||08:45 AM||||Thanks, great advise here..  Sorry if I may be doing a 180 degree turn here, but how bad are the stock Fiero brakes.  I keep going back and fourth about whether to do the Grand Am conversion or rebuild the existing brakes.  The reason I'm asking about the performance of the stock brakes is that my Fiero has 150k miles, with holes in the brake lines and worn out pads it doesn't make for great stopping. However, with the stock brakes in peak condition how 'bad' are they.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||01-11-2001||09:01 AM||||I haven't put the pre '88 brakes through much, but yesterday, a lady in an S-10 pulled right out in front of me while I was doing 50+mph(not speeding). There was more than enough braking power in my '88 to lock them up, and by the time I slid beside the lady still going about 10mph, I smelled the distinct smell of burning rubber, and lots of tire smoke came billowing in front of me. I was impressed, but I still need to do something about my barely functioning rear brakes.<BR>From my experiences, the vented '88 brakes are fine, but I haven't had a chance to heat up the brakes on previous years. Hopefully soon when I title/register my '86.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Racer 1||01-11-2001||10:55 AM||||Now what do you all think about these brakes at the <B>fiero store</B>? Are they grand am type?  <IMG SRC="http://www.secureleadercom.com/store/images/56004.GIF"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.secureleadercom.com/store/images/56004+.GIF"> <P>They run $289.95 with $60.00 core charge.<P>And they have this brake master cyld  <IMG SRC="http://www.secureleadercom.com/store/images/56057.GIF">  $49.95<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://spitfire.cwv.net/~cdbailey/images/gifssucl/eyes2/evil_eyes.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000009||theogre||01-11-2001||07:35 PM||||My oppinion is that Fiero Store has simply followed the same seriously flawed upgrade instructions as everyone else. I think they saw something they thought would make a buck on and didn't do half the research they needed to. I don't believe they have adressed the mechanical issues Will mentioned above or the hydraulic problems. <P>Their own statement that the Blazer MC increases brake power is wrong on it's face. Increasing MC piston size decreases power at the caliper. When used with OE Fiero rear brakes the reduction is alarmingly large.<P>No, I don't believe anyone else offering this type "upgrade kit" has adiquately dealt with these issues either. Anyone offering these as a kit should consult an a attourny and a liability insurance company very carefully. They are looking at major lawsuits if anyone is hurt in or by a car equiped with this.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-11-2001).]||||reg||1
